# MALLOC(3)

## NAME

malloc, free, realloc, calloc, aligned_alloc - main memory allocator

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

void *malloc(size_t size);

void free(void *ptr);

void *realloc(void *ptr, size_t size);

void *calloc(size_t nelem, size_t elsize);

void *aligned_alloc(size_t alignment, size_t size);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Malloc* and *free* provide a simple general-purpose memory allocation package. *Malloc*
returns a pointer to a block of at least *size* bytes beginning on a word boundary.

The argument to *free* is a pointer to a block previously allocated by *malloc*; this space is
made available for further allocation, but its contents are left undisturbed. **Note:** a null
*ptr* is accepted and does nothing, which is C11 sec 7.22.3.3. v7 would have walked the arena
from a wild address.

Needless to say, grave disorder will result if the space assigned by *malloc* is overrun or if
some random number is handed to *free*.

*Malloc* allocates the first big enough contiguous reach of free space found in a circular
search from the last block allocated or freed, coalescing adjacent free blocks as it searches.
It calls *sbrk* (see break(2)) to get more memory from the system when there is no suitable
space already free.

*Realloc* changes the size of the block pointed to by *ptr* to *size* bytes and returns a
pointer to the (possibly moved) block. The contents will be unchanged up to the lesser of the
new and old sizes. **Note:** a null *ptr* makes it a *malloc*, per C11 sec 7.22.3.5.

*Realloc* also works if *ptr* points to a block freed since the last call of *malloc, realloc*
or *calloc*; thus sequences of *free, malloc* and *realloc* can exploit the search strategy of
*malloc* to do storage compaction.

*Calloc* allocates space for an array of *nelem* elements of size *elsize.* The space is
initialized to zeros. **Note:** the product is checked for overflow, which v7 did not do.

**Note:** *aligned_alloc* is C11 and had no v7 ancestor. It is *malloc* with its *alignment*
argument checked. **An alignment wider than a word is not supported:** every allocation is
already word-aligned, and there is no unit finer than a word to align to, so the routine
accepts an alignment of one word or less and refuses anything else.

Each of the allocation routines returns a pointer to space suitably aligned (after possible
pointer coercion) for storage of any type of object.

## DIAGNOSTICS

*Malloc, realloc, calloc* and *aligned_alloc* return a null pointer (0) if there is no
available memory or if the arena has been detectably corrupted by storing outside the bounds of
a block.

**Note:** v7 said *malloc* "may be recompiled to check the arena very stringently on every
transaction; see the source code." That **#ifdef debug** scaffolding is *gone,* not merely
compiled out. Its one structural trace is in the free-list search, where two nested tests whose
else branch was an assertion collapse into a single condition.

## BUGS

When *realloc* returns 0, the block pointed to by *ptr* may be destroyed.

## BESM-6 NOTES

### Where the BUSY bit lives

This is the one change to the algorithm, and it is forced.

v7 packs the flag into **bit 0** of a block's link, which is free on a byte-addressed machine
because its list cell is two bytes wide there and every block is even. **Here a block is
exactly one word and an address is a word index,** so adjacent blocks differ by 1 and bit 0 is
a *significant* address bit - setting it would name the next block.

The flag moves *above* the address instead, to **bit 16**: a regular (non-fat) pointer carries
its 15-bit word address in bits 15..1 with bits 48..16 zero, so bit 16 is free, one past the
top of the address space, and it sits far below the bit-48 marker that would make a marked link
look like a fat **char \***.

Nothing else in the algorithm notices - v7 already clears the flag before every dereference and
every comparison - and the casts it takes are free, pointer and integer being one word each.

### The break is granted a page at a time

So the growth chunk and the step the allocator backs off by when refused are both a page (1024
words). Anything smaller asks for exactly what was just given, or exactly what was just
refused.

Sbrk(2) fails with **NULL**, not v7's **(char \*)-1**, which would have to be fabricated out of
an integer; *malloc* tests for NULL.

### A size is checked before it becomes a word count

A **size_t** is 48 bits wide and a word address is 15, so an unchecked byte size would wrap
when divided into words. There is an up-front guard for it.

Note also that *calloc*'s overflow guard must *not* be written **(size_t)-1**, which on this
machine is 2**41-1 rather than 2**48-1: a signed-to-unsigned conversion is a bare copy of a
41-bit pattern, not an adjustment by 2**48.

### realloc's two failure returns are kept apart

v7 writes them as one **return (char \*)q**. Casting a *null* word pointer to a fat one
produces a pointer with the bit-48 marker and a byte offset set over a zero address - a null
that is not a zero word. The failure path returns a plain NULL instead.

### This is not the c-compiler's allocator

That one claims the whole span from *end* to the stack on first use, which would defeat the
kernel's demand-grown break and leave nothing between the heap and the stack.

